  "text": "Shape-shifting is the power to move between bodies. In Celtic myth it belongs to bards and druids. They could change their form and keep their essence. The poet Amairgin, first poet of the Milesians, spoke a long verse on entering Ireland. He named himself a wave of the sea and a salmon in a pool. Traditional lists live in the poems of Tuan Mac Cairill and the Welsh Taliesin. Enchantment could also force a change. Sadb, mother of the poet Oisín, was turned into a deer. A spiteful fairy made the beauty Aige a doe. The cruel Aífe was changed into a crane after she cursed her kin to live as swans for 900 years. Some shifters chose their form. Étain and Midir left Tara as swans. Cian became a pig to hide from the Sons of Tuireann. The Welsh wizard Ceridwen shifted many times to catch her thieving servant Gwion. Witches took the shape of hares to steal milk and butter. A witch struck as a hare would limp the next day.",
